[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I put a design together.

  [1mWhere the notification actually appears.[0m This is the one remaining choice that changes the architecture rather than
  just the styling:

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a bell/badge in the header plus a panel listing due and overdue tasks. Pure DOM, no permissions, no
    browser APIs. Works only while the tab is open and focused enough to look at. Nothing to fall back to, because
    there's nothing that can fail.
  - [1mIn-page + OS notifications[0m — same panel, plus the browser [38;5;153mNotification[39m API so an alert surfaces when the tab is
    backgrounded. Requires a permission prompt, must degrade cleanly when the user denies it (so the in-page panel is
    still the source of truth, not an optional extra). Still dies when the tab is [3mclosed[0m — real background delivery
    needs a service worker plus push infrastructure, which means a server, which we ruled out.
  - [1mOS notifications only[0m — no in-page surface. Doesn't work if permission is denied, and leaves no way to see what
    you've already missed. I'd advise against it.

  Cheap to change later: adding the OS layer on top of a working in-page panel is genuinely additive — the permission
  flow and the [38;5;153mNotification[39m call sit behind one small adapter. Expensive: nothing here, as long as the in-page panel is
  the source of truth.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m in-page only for the first pass, built so the OS layer is a drop-in later. The permission prompt is
  a real cost to a user who hasn't yet seen the feature do anything useful, and I'd rather earn it.
